[Music]  the war in ukraine continues but in this  week's fyi we learned that children in  russia are being told there isn't a war  and from inside the conflict we have an  exclusive report from nadia she is one  of the thousands of children who was  forced to take shelter  i got scared at first i didn't  understand what was going on  and we meet the ukrainian children who  live in the uk who are helping refugees  this is fyi your weekly news show now  imagine we told you that there's no war  going on in ukraine and that it's all  rubbish would you believe us well that's  what children in russia are being told  we've been investigating how fake news  and misinformation is being used by the  russian government and why  since the russian president vladimir  putin decided to invade ukraine  he has never admitted to starting a war  he claims that he sent in troops as part  of a special security operation world  leaders have dismissed his claims saying  that president putin had started an  illegal full-scale invasion of an  independent country it now seems the  russian authorities are trying to  mislead people living in russia by using  propaganda videos and news bulletins  propaganda is a bit like fake news it's  misleading information to create a  particular opinion  we found a video that is being aimed at  children to convince them that there  isn't a war in ukraine  we are talking about the events in  ukraine the russian ministry of  education video is presented by a famous  12 year old russian singer who is joined  by adult presenters  the ukraine crisis didn't start  yesterday and it is not just about  ukraine there are many images about a  war in ukraine but actually these are  from other war conflicts  some images are even from computer games  as well as trying to convince russians  that the footage of the invasion is fake  the russian government is shutting down  radio and tv stations that have reported  the truth  this is the moment that staff from a tv  station had to walk offset after their  final broadcast they claimed the russian  government closed them down because they  referred to the ukraine invasion as a  war  now the russian people only have access  to government-run tv and radio stations  social media platforms and news stations  outside russia like the bbc are being  blocked from broadcasting in the country  many russians are against their  country's invasion of ukraine but those  who turn out on the streets to protest  are being arrested  all because they said no to war  [Music]  if you want to find out more about why  russia invaded ukraine and how world  leaders are trying to bring a peaceful  end to the war check out our i don't get  it explainer just head to our webpage  first.news fyi there are still many  ukrainians trying to flee the country  but it's getting more difficult  day after day russia has promised  ceasefires so that refugees could leave  towns and cities safely but in many  cases the fighting has continued in an  exclusive report nadia wanted to share  her story on fyi she is safe now but at  the start of the invasion nadi was being  treated in children's hospital in the  capital city kiev  nadia was in the middle of receiving  cancer treatment in hospital when she  heard the news about the war  the beginning of the war for me all  started when i woke up at about six in  the morning my mum was lying on the bed  next to mine and watching something  loudly  i got up and grabbed my phone  i saw that my friend was writing  something to me  and instead of good morning nadia she  wrote  nadia the war  i live near the airport it was blown up  by russian soldiers  i got scared at first i didn't  understand what was going on  i thought is this true or not  i went back into the room and i saw  zelensky was saying something on tv  my mum was watching and she was like  don't worry everything will be fine  and i understood that things are no  longer good  and i started shaking a little like this  i laid down on my bed and tried to fall  asleep  knowing that something was wrong  i heard some kind of strange sound i  said mum can you hear that there's some  noise outside the window  nothing exploded everything was fine  just terrible sirens  a nurse came up and said take the most  important documents your passport  maybe if the siren doesn't stop  screaming we'll have to go down to the  basement we'll take all sorts of things  downstairs just in case something  happens things like food water  mattresses they took everything  as the war escalated nadia and her  mother were then stuck in the hospital  basement for 10 days  only a couple of days before all this i  was sitting and telling my mum my plans  i said mum we will be released from  hospital on monday also then we'll go  home and take the money and buy me some  watercolors some other paints and all  sorts of canvas  after an emergency evacuation some of  the patients at the children's hospital  are now on their way to safety  nadia is heading to a hospital in italy  where she can continue receiving  treatment  refugee charities save four to seven  million ukrainian people are expected to  flee the fighting in ukraine  with just a few belongings many are  escaping to neighboring countries that's  moldova romania hungary slovakia and the  majority are heading into poland  but what do you do when you leave your  home and everything you have behind well  refugees and those trying to help them  wanted to share their story on fyi  [Music]  more than a million ukrainians have fled  to poland since the russian president  vladimir putin decided to invade ukraine  we met a student called kate near the  border between the two countries and she  explained how worried she was about the  family members she'd had to leave behind  i tried to  text them every day every hour because  they're very scary message from news  channels in kiev it's like  a key over bombs  it's very scary  there's been a lot of criticism that the  uk government hasn't done enough to help  the refugees come in here with  complaints that the process is too slow  and too difficult  but the people of poland have been  praised for rushing to help ukrainian  refugees  at the border crossing we met zuzana who  is part of the polish scouts and one of  the thousands of polish people who are  helping out  so we are organizing them places where  they can sleep or rest and we're giving  them food coats or things that they're  needed for them  but it's not just at the border that  people have been helping out families  all over poland have been offering to  give refugees a place to stay  hello fyi my name is gabrielle i'm from  krakow in poland  is just 240 kilometers away from ukraine  one of the things i love about this  place is that everyone is so friendly  and welcoming  so when we saw people fleeing here from  ukraine we knew we had to act  it was important for me and for them  because they had to rest and talk about  what to do next  i am very sorry that everything has been  ruined  [Music]  all the refugees who came to find a new  home in poland  some of these people have seen truly  horrible things on the way to my city  they are very worried about their  families who were left in ukraine to  fight  the family who've been staying with us  have now got a new home in katowice  we are taking them supplies to help them  establish themselves  the least we can do is open our homes  and our hearts the people of ukraine  because they do need our help  these are our neighbors  and they really need our support  [Music]  there are thousands of ukrainian  children that live in the uk  many of them have friends or family  either still in ukraine or trying to  flee their homeland understandably  they're super worried about them  every saturday lots of young ukrainians  go to saint mary's ukrainian school in  london  they told fyi about the things they're  doing to help their families and the  people of ukraine  [Music]  we're trying to support ukraine in any  way we can from here in england  it's important in the fact that there  are people dying there are people  struggling it's not just the fact that  it's our country as friends as family  but it's also there's people living in  bomb shelters people  under attack and it's basically our duty  to help them  i grew up in ukraine i was born there i  spent like most of my childhood there  so it's very close to my heart and it  hurts to see somewhere i grew up to be  destroyed  it's just being destroyed and  we've  done nothing to like deserve that  we need to  remember all the people right now in  ukraine  and what they're going through right now  we're all worried about our  other family members in ukraine maybe  something happens to them  and  we don't know  my grandma called me today and said that  she could go out to buy some things  like they have a siren that tells them  when they can go out and where they have  to stay in  we've been making posters and making  these ribbons that we could just show to  the public to raise awareness i've been  in school today and we've been doing  some  stuff to raise money to help ukraine  [Music]  we made a poster with all of the main  parts of ukraine just to show like we  want them to stay in ukraine because  it's our homeland  sunflower is a symbol of ukraine  so it's quite important because of the  war  right now  right now it's  very important for us to be united with  our friends with our family and with our  community  russia is not only waging  a physical war in ukraine by an  informational one as well and i think  it's really important to know what is  going on there  down at the russian embassy it's not  only ukrainians there are lots of other  people who actually come there and  support  you could support you could donate  just help ukraine because  we don't want to be taken over by russia  it's nice to know in ukraine people who  are there that all over the world there  are people who are going to support them  there are people who are standing with  them every step of the way because if  i'm just standing there listening all  i'm going to do is worry but if i come  here and if i try and help if i try and  donate if i try and protest if i try and  make posters it feels like you're doing  something you're actually helping  and that makes you feel a bit better  [Music]  but it's not just ukrainian families  that live in the uk there are thousands  of russian families that live here too  now many of them are against the war but  they're worried that russian children  will be discriminated against or bullied  at school because of the war  we've had lots of questions come in from  fy viewers who would like advice about  this we're joined by child psychologist  laverne antrobus to answer some of your  concerns thank you for joining us  laverne thank you for having me so lots  of us know people from ukrainian or  russian families but we're not too sure  what to say to them like kira who sent  us this question  what should we say to people at school  who have russian or ukrainian families  thank you kyra that's a really  thoughtful question i mean it is  complicated isn't it when you're faced  with these very difficult situations but  i suppose you know what we do know is  that the russian president and his  generals have invaded ukraine it's not  necessarily as we know something that  all russians support and so being on  hand for both sets of your friends is  really important so what do you do if  someone's being bullied or making an  inappropriate comment about the war  i think that there's nothing that's  changed about bullying you have to call  it out you have to talk to your parents  or to your teachers about this but i  think you've also got to remember to  talk to the person that's being bullied  and offer them your support and as for  inappropriate comments i mean i'd want  people to feel that they could be sort  of have a bit of authority here to say  that's not helpful you know this is a  really difficult time that people are  going through so let's all think about  what we say because our words do have an  impact well that's really great advice  so our next question is from seraphina  what do you do when the news is just  upsetting you so much  thanks seraphina gosh really really  important question isn't how do you stay  informed and involved but also look  after yourself i think it's about  choosing your news very carefully maybe  checking in in the right places so fyi  is a brilliant place i think there are  some ordinary things to remember you  know taking yourself off social media  just before bedtime you don't want to go  to bed with sort of lots of overwhelming  thoughts in your minds and i think  alongside that you know sometimes we can  stumble across things just accidentally  that really are quite distressing and  quite upsetting so if that happens you  know talk to somebody about it so that  you're not having to manage this sort of  awful images that can sort of take up  our minds um on your own so talk to a  parent or talk to your friends i'm sure  a lot of what you've said there has been  really helpful for loads of viewers  watching this now so thank you so much  for your advice and thank you so much  for your time well thanks for having me  well we know that the worst situations  like war can also bring out the best in  people and people all over the world are  standing with ukraine  regular view with pictures of everyone  coming together in the hope that soon  there will be peace  [Music]  you

